An oriented graph is a directed graph $D=(V,E)$ with no loops and no pair of opposite arcs (i.e., for any two distinct vertices $u,v$, at most one of the arcs $(u,v)$, $(v,u)$ belongs to $E$). For a vertex $v \in V$, define:
- the (first) out-neighborhood $N^+(v) = \{u \in V : (v,u) \in E\}$;
- the in-neighborhood $N^-(v) = \{u \in V : (u,v) \in E\}$;
- the second out-neighborhood $N^{++}(v) = \{u \in V : u \notin N^+(v) \cup \{v\}$ and there exists $w \in N^+(v)$ with $(w,u) \in E\}$, i.e., the set of vertices at directed distance exactly $2$ from $v$.

Prove or disprove: every finite oriented graph $D$ contains a vertex $v$ such that
$$|N^{++}(v)| \ge |N^-(v)|.$$

